<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>

<!--
    MainScene - the whole UI.

    Debugging note: this file is the product of a hardware bisect. See README
    "Two gates, and neither is sufficient" for why BrighterScript being clean
    means very little here. Two sections there cover attributes that are
    documented, XSD-valid, and absent below on purpose: "LabelList font crashes
    the render thread" (the PosterGrid's caption fonts) and "a list is a single
    column, always" (why the tab bar is Labels and not a list).
-->
<component name="MainScene" extends="Scene">
    <!-- The entire bridge to the main thread. These are a direct child of
         <component>, NOT of <script>: the docs show <interface> standalone and
         the XSD rejects children on <script> (error BS1069). -->
    <interface>
        <field id="apiRequest" type="assocarray" />
        <field id="apiResult" type="assocarray" onChange="onApiResult" />
    </interface>

    <script type="text/brightscript" uri="pkg:/components/MainScene.brs" />

    <children>
        <!-- ============================================ COLORS
             Tokyo Night Storm. Every color in this file comes from
             that palette.

             The literals are 0xRRGGBBAA - ALPHA IS THE LAST BYTE.
             This is the reverse of every other convention in common
             use (CSS, Android, PIL, .NET Color.FromArgb) and getting
             it backwards is invisible to every gate in this project:
             the XSD types this attribute as
             color_string_containing_hex_value_eg_RGBA but never checks
             the order, brighterscript has no color parser at all and
             passes the literal straight through, and `npm run check`
             stays clean. It is only visible on a TV, where an
             alpha-first 0xFFRRGGBB renders as a nearly transparent
             full-strength red and the whole UI looks broken.

             Confirmed three ways against
             devtools.web.roku.com/schema/RokuSceneGraph.xsd: the Scene
             backgroundColor default is 0x000000FF (black at full
             alpha, which read alpha-first would be invisible), every
             opaque default ends in ff, and optionsColor/optionsDimColor
             are 0xddddddff / 0xdddddd44 - same RGB, dimmed purely by
             the trailing byte. scene.md's own example,
             scene.backgroundColor="0xEB1010FF", is a red.

             The one literal that is not FF-terminated is the play
             scrim: 0x24283BC0, the surface hue at 75% alpha.

               surface          #24283B   storm editor background
               primary text     #C0CAF5   storm foreground
               secondary text   #A9B1D6   storm editor foreground
               dim text         #9AA5CE   storm markdown/html text
               muted / chrome   #565F89   storm comment
               accent / focus   #7AA2F7   storm blue

             The text ramp is four steps and each one is a real slot in
             the palette, not a brightened or darkened interpolation of
             one. Brightness descends monotonically with importance:
             primary 9.02:1, secondary 6.90:1, dim 6.00:1, muted 2.35:1,
             against the #24283B surface. The accent is 5.78:1, and the
             play scrim is 13:1 even composited over a white frame.

             #565F89 is below the 3:1 legibility floor and is meant to
             be: it carries the server URL and the control hints, which
             are looked at last and read once. Anything that must be
             noticed - the selected tab, a video title, a failure - sits
             at 5.78:1 or better.
             ================================================ -->

        <Font id="fontSmall" size="20" />
        <Font id="fontStatus" size="24" />
        <Font id="fontTab" size="28" />
        <Font id="fontPlayTitle" size="34" />
        <Font id="fontPlayStatus" size="24" />

        <!-- The Video node's `state` field is sampled rather than observed, so
             it needs something to tick it. Note the field is `duration` in
             seconds, not `interval`. The main thread is the only place a
             blocking Wait() is safe, which is why this is a Timer and not a
             loop in onApiResult. -->
        <Timer id="stateTimer" repeat="true" duration="0.5" />

        <Rectangle id="background" width="1920" height="1080" color="0x24283BFF" />

        <!-- Mode picker. THREE FOCUSABLE LABELS IN A GROUP, not a
             LabelList, and the reason is not a style choice.

             Roku's "Lists and grids" reference is explicit: "Lists are a
             single column of items arranged vertically", and ArrayGrid's
             numColumns is documented as "This field is not used for lists".
             There is no attribute that turns a LabelList into a horizontal
             strip. numRows is the number of VISIBLE rows, so numRows="1"
             does not turn the items sideways - it makes the node a
             one-row-tall window onto a vertical list.

             The previous LabelList had exactly that: a 300x56 window at
             [96,44] that showed one of the three tab names at a time and
             scrolled the other two out of view, and being a list it
             swallowed every up/down press, so the focus could land on it
             and never leave. Both symptoms, one wrong node type.

             Labels have none of those problems. Each has a real width and
             height, so nothing is clipped, and they do not consume arrow
             keys, so the focus can move both ways. Left/right/ok/down are
             handled explicitly in onKeyEvent rather than left to the focus
             chain, so the wrap is ours; see TabWidth(), focusTab() and
             focusedTab() in MainScene.brs.

             Two indicators, because selection and focus are different
             things - the user arrows onto a tab before committing to it
             with OK. tabMarker, the accent underline, marks the SELECTED
             tab; text brightness marks the tab holding the key focus. Both
             are maintained by paintTabs().

             The color on each Label is only a resting value: init() runs
             paintTabs() before show(), so the script owns these before the
             first frame. The literal in the script is the same
             0xRRGGBBAA (alpha last) dim tier as everywhere else.

             A Font on a Label is safe. The hardware bisect that ruled out
             font= on a LabelList and caption fonts on a PosterGrid (see
             README, "LabelList font crashes the render thread") left every
             Label alone, which is why the tab bar can have a real font now
             that it is made of Labels. -->
        <Group id="tabs" translation="[96, 44]">
            <Label
                id="tab0"
                width="220"
                height="48"
                focusable="true"
                font="fontTab"
                color="0x9AA5CEFF"
                horizAlign="center"
                vertAlign="center"
                text="Videos" />
            <Label
                id="tab1"
                translation="[220, 0]"
                width="220"
                height="48"
                focusable="true"
                color="0x9AA5CEFF"
                horizAlign="center"
                vertAlign="center"
                text="Pins" />
            <Label
                id="tab2"
                translation="[440, 0]"
                width="220"
                height="48"
                focusable="true"
                color="0x9AA5CEFF"
                horizAlign="center"
                vertAlign="center"
                text="Search" />
            <Rectangle
                id="tabMarker"
                translation="[0, 52]"
                width="220"
                height="4"
                color="0x7AA2F7FF" />
        </Group>

        <Label
            id="serverLabel"
            translation="[96, 116]"
            width="1728"
            height="30"
            color="0x565F89FF"
            text="" />

        <Label
            id="statusLabel"
            translation="[96, 172]"
            width="1728"
            height="40"
            color="0xFFFFFFFF"
            text="" />

        <!-- Wrapper so the browser and the player can be swapped without
             each one touching the other's visibility. -->
        <Group id="browseGroup">
            <!-- No width/height either: the grid derives its extent from
                 numColumns/numRows, basePosterSize and itemSpacing, so
                 translation is all that positions it. Unlike a list, a grid
                 does use both values of itemSpacing.

                 NO caption1Font / caption2Font, and that is not an oversight.
                 Attaching a Font to a list or grid item renderer kills the
                 render thread on this firmware (Roku OS 15.3.4) as soon as the
                 node has CONTENT, with a silent EXIT_SYSTEM_KILL and no
                 BrightScript error. Confirmed on hardware: this PosterGrid
                 died about five seconds in, right after the first page of 50
                 videos was appended, and an earlier LabelList tab bar died the
                 moment it was given three items. With no font attached, both
                 survive populated. Label nodes are unaffected - every font in
                 this file is on a Label, which is also why the tab bar above
                 is Labels. Captions therefore fall back to the system
                 default. -->
            <PosterGrid
                id="grid"
                translation="[144, 244]"
                numColumns="6"
                numRows="3"
                basePosterSize="[252, 142]"
                itemSpacing="[24, 28]"
                posterDisplayMode="scaleToFit"
                useAtlas="false"
                caption1Color="0xC0CAF5FF"
                caption1NumLines="2"
                caption2Color="0xA9B1D6FF"
                caption2NumLines="1"
                captionHorizAlignment="left"
                captionVertAlignment="below"
                enableCaptionScrolling="false" />
        </Group>

        <Group id="playGroup" visible="false">
            <!-- No width/height, deliberately: a Video node with neither set
                 defaults to the screen, so the picture takes the screen's width
                 and height in whatever display mode the TV is in. The previous
                 explicit width="1920" height="1080" did not match, and the
                 picture was not filling the screen. What the screen actually
                 measures is not recorded here because it could not be read back
                 from the device - see below - and hardcoding a guess is what
                 caused the bug in the first place. Do not put the numbers back.

                 There is also no ScalingMode attribute: it is not a field of a
                 Video node, and setting it only logs "Tried to set nonexistent
                 field "ScalingMode" of a "Video" node". The node scaling the
                 picture to its own bounds is what does the fitting.

                 The screen size is deliberately not hardcoded anywhere. It
                 cannot be read back: m.top.width, the scene node's width from
                 the main thread, and roDeviceInfo.GetDisplaySize()'s keys all
                 come back invalid on this firmware, and roDeviceInfo's array
                 exposes neither GetKeys nor usable indices. Letting the node
                 default to the screen is the one approach that cannot be wrong
                 for whatever display mode the TV is in. The overlay below is
                 still laid out in explicit 1920x1080 coordinates, which is the
                 open question if the UI turns out to need a different design
                 size. -->
            <Video id="video" focusable="true" />
        </Group>

        <!-- Deliberately NOT a child of playGroup. A playing Video node is
             composited above its later siblings, so an overlay declared inside
             the same group is drawn underneath the picture and never seen -
             which is exactly what happened: the title and status were set
             correctly (verified over sgnodes while a video was playing) and
             still invisible. Declared here, after playGroup, it paints on top. -->
        <Group id="playOverlay" visible="false">
            <!-- A bottom scrim, NOT a full-screen curtain, so the picture stays
                 visible. The trailing C0 is 75% alpha, which keeps the primary
                 text legible over a bright frame without going fully black -
                 still 13:1 against white. Same hue as the surface, so the
                 overlay does not drift out of tone. -->
            <Rectangle translation="[0, 690]" width="1920" height="390" color="0x24283BC0" />
            <Label
                id="playTitle"
                translation="[160, 730]"
                width="1600"
                height="110"
                color="0xC0CAF5FF"
                maxLines="2"
                text="" />
            <Label
                id="playStatus"
                translation="[160, 862]"
                width="1600"
                height="50"
                color="0xA9B1D6FF"
                text="" />
        </Group>

        <Label
            id="hintLabel"
            translation="[96, 1014]"
            width="1728"
            height="32"
            font="fontSmall"
            color="0x565F89FF"
            text="up tabs      OK play      back leave      * search      options server" />
    </children>
</component>
